  "preliminary_interpretation": {
    "confirmed": [
      "The unchanged official Test 2 passed: 50 successful purchases, 350 refusals, no errors or overselling, 21.26 seconds of reported fire() wall time.",
      "In seven samples, the only ungranted tuple target was public.products at exactly the physical location of the visible official synthetic fixture row. Up to 323 requests waited for ExclusiveLock on that matched tuple.",
      "Transaction-ID waits requested ShareLock and corresponding granted holders had ExclusiveLock. Transaction-ID targets carry no table relation, so those targets were not relabeled as product-row locks.",
      "No ungranted relation lock or other heavyweight lock type was sampled. Granted relation locks are context, not evidence of blocked tables.",
      "pg_blocking_pids returned a dense queue graph. Most matched relationships corresponded to an earlier ungranted request on the same target, rather than a granted holder.",
      "Fixture safety passed before and after; original race/source hashes and database function/index/trigger fingerprints were unchanged."
    ],
    "hypotheses_and_limits": [
      "The six unmatched product tuple locations in the first blocking sample may be earlier fixture row versions, but this was not established by a visible fixture tuple match.",
      "Fixture-row contention contributes to waiting; its contribution to total end-to-end wall time and whether it is the primary bottleneck remain unquantified.",
      "The 21.26-second run cannot be causally compared with prior 12.89/16.10-second runs. This sampler calls pg_blocking_pids repeatedly and inspects pg_locks, which can add lock-manager contention.",
      "There is timing drift within samples: 57 pair observations lacked matching lock-target context and 208 pair observations had a waiter whose earlier activity snapshot was not in Lock.",
      "Recorded wait ages are repeated samples of elapsed time since pg_locks.waitstart, not completed per-call wait durations; sources are not globally atomic.",
      "Query round-trip timing is the authoritative monitor duration; SQL clock markers delimit parts of evaluation and should not be interpreted as exact simultaneous acquisition of all views."
    ]
